What:		/sys/bus/platform/devices/<unit>.sep/diag/boot
Date:		October 2026
KernelVersion:	7.1
Contact:	Chris Kearney <chris@kearneymail.com>
Description:
		(RO) How the driver hands its shared memory to the SEP, as
		the platform profile sets it. Fixed at probe.

		====	=====================================================
		cold	The driver boots the SEP and hands the memory over
			during that boot.
		warm	The boot firmware left the SEP running, and the
			driver registers the memory with it in one message.
		====	=====================================================

What:		/sys/bus/platform/devices/<unit>.sep/diag/protocol
Date:		October 2026
KernelVersion:	7.1
Contact:	Chris Kearney <chris@kearneymail.com>
Description:
		(RO) The key-store protocol the driver speaks to the SEP, as
		the platform profile sets it. It is not read from the SEP,
		and it does not identify a sepOS build. Fixed at probe.

		========	=============================================
		sepos13		The sepOS 13.5 key store, whose endpoint the
				driver initialises before first use.
		variant5	The newer key store, which creates keybags
				with the variant-5 request.
		========	=============================================

What:		/sys/bus/platform/devices/<unit>.sep/diag/xart
Date:		October 2026
KernelVersion:	7.1
Contact:	Chris Kearney <chris@kearneymail.com>
Description:
		(RO) Whether the SEP's writes to the shared xART store reach
		the disk. Set at probe, when the driver opens the store;
		complete_attach updates it when it reopens the store, and a
		failed writable reopen there sets it to disabled.

		========	=============================================
		enabled		Writes reach the store (it validated for
				writing).
		disabled	Writes are refused: the key-store-only
				initrd phase before complete_attach
				(keystore_only=1, or 2 with the xART
				guard), or a failed writable reopen.
				Touch ID enrol and verify are refused
				too, since the SEP must save state after
				each. There is no parameter that turns
				writes off.
		========	=============================================

What:		/sys/bus/platform/devices/<unit>.sep/diag/attach
Date:		October 2026
KernelVersion:	7.1
Contact:	Chris Kearney <chris@kearneymail.com>
Description:
		(RO) Whether the SEP answered the driver's attach.

		========	=============================================
		pending		No outcome yet.
		attached	The SEP advertised at least one endpoint.
		failed		The SEP advertised no endpoint; the xART
				exchange and the key store cannot run this
				boot.
		========	=============================================

		The driver decides once about three seconds have passed
		without an endpoint and the SEP has gone quiet. With boot
		"warm" that wait starts at probe, so a SEP that never answers
		ends in "failed". With boot "cold" it starts at the first
		message from the SEP, so a SEP that never answers the boot
		request leaves this at "pending" for the rest of the boot.

What:		/sys/bus/platform/devices/<unit>.sep/diag/endpoints
Date:		October 2026
KernelVersion:	7.1
Contact:	Chris Kearney <chris@kearneymail.com>
Description:
		(RO) The number of distinct endpoints the SEP has
		advertised, as an unsigned decimal. It reads "0" until
		attach reads "attached". Discovery goes on after the attach,
		so the number can still grow afterwards, and can exceed the
		count the attach message in the kernel log reports.

What:		/sys/bus/platform/devices/<unit>.sep/diag/keystore
Date:		October 2026
KernelVersion:	7.1
Contact:	Chris Kearney <chris@kearneymail.com>
Description:
		(RO) Whether the boot-time bring-up opened the key store.

		=======	==============================================
		unknown	The bring-up has not decided yet.
		open	The key-store endpoint is enabled and, where the
			protocol needs it, initialised.
		closed	The bring-up ended without opening it. Keybag and
			Touch ID operations are unavailable this boot.
		=======	==============================================

What:		/sys/bus/platform/devices/<unit>.sep/diag/keybag
Date:		October 2026
KernelVersion:	7.1
Contact:	Chris Kearney <chris@kearneymail.com>
Description:
		(RO) The identity keybag that Touch ID uses.

		=======	==============================================
		unknown	Not determined yet.
		present	An identity keybag exists: one was already
			persisted, or the bring-up created one. This does
			not say that it loads; Touch ID activation loads
			it, and changes this to "failed" if that fails.
		missing	None is persisted and none was provisioned.
		failed	Provisioning, reading or loading it failed.
		=======	==============================================

		The boot-time bring-up decides it once the key store is
		open, and it stays "unknown" if the key store never opens.

What:		/sys/bus/platform/devices/<unit>.sep/diag/sensor
Date:		October 2026
KernelVersion:	7.1
Contact:	Chris Kearney <chris@kearneymail.com>
Description:
		(RO) The fingerprint sensor on its SPI bus. The driver
		creates the sensor device when Touch ID is activated, and
		brings the sensor up then and again before each enrol or
		verify capture.

		=======	==============================================
		unbound	No SPI sensor device is bound.
		bound	The sensor is bound and has not been brought up
			since it bound.
		online	The most recent bring-up succeeded.
		failed	The most recent bring-up failed.
		=======	==============================================

What:		/sys/bus/platform/devices/<unit>.sep/diag/touchid
Date:		October 2026
KernelVersion:	7.1
Contact:	Chris Kearney <chris@kearneymail.com>
Description:
		(RO) Whether the Touch ID sensor side is ready to enrol and
		verify. Whether the enclave may save the state a match
		writes, which the xART guard can refuse, is in matching.

		=========	============================================
		unknown		Still in progress: the boot-time bring-up has
				not finished, or /dev/sep-bio is published
				but has not been opened yet, or activation
				is still running.
		ready		/dev/sep-bio is published, Touch ID was
				activated, and the most recent sensor
				bring-up succeeded.
		not-ready	Touch ID was activated, but the sensor is not
				online: it is not bound, has not been brought
				up since it bound, or its most recent
				bring-up failed. A later bring-up, which
				each enrol and verify runs, can still make
				it ready.
		failed		Touch ID cannot work this boot: the bring-up
				ended without publishing /dev/sep-bio, or
				activation failed. Neither is retried before
				the next boot.
		=========	============================================

		There is no read-only install: the xart_writes and
		provision_keybag parameters were removed, so the bring-up
		always provisions a missing identity keybag before it
		publishes /dev/sep-bio, and "failed" is a real failure.

		Because the first open of /dev/sep-bio activates Touch ID, a
		tool waiting for "ready" has to open it, or let its client
		do so, first. Enrol and verify also need xart to read
		"enabled".

What:		/sys/bus/platform/devices/<unit>.sep/diag/sensor_attach
Date:		October 2026
KernelVersion:	7.1
Contact:	Ryan Murray <ryan@aurorasilicon.org>
Description:
		(RO) How far the automatic attach of the fingerprint sensor
		got. Touch ID activation brings the sensor up, with spaced
		retries, and if that falls short one more round follows
		about 10 s later, once the boot-time bring-up is over.

		=========	============================================
		unknown		Touch ID has not been activated, or its
				attach has not decided yet.
		attached	The sensor came online and the enclave's
				identity view was proven; restore says what
				became of the enrolled fingerprints.
		retrying	The attach-time bring-up fell short and the
				later round has still to run.
		not-ready	Every automatic bring-up fell short. Capture
				is unavailable until an enrol or verify
				brings the sensor up.
		=========	============================================

		touchid follows every later bring-up, while this attribute
		describes the attach alone.

What:		/sys/bus/platform/devices/<unit>.sep/diag/restore
Date:		October 2026
KernelVersion:	7.1
Contact:	Ryan Murray <ryan@aurorasilicon.org>
Description:
		(RO) What became of the enrolled fingerprints when the sensor
		was first attached this boot. Decided once, by the first
		bring-up that reaches the enclave's identity view.

		========	============================================
		unknown		No bring-up has reached the identity view.
		restored	The enclave lists the enrolled fingerprints
				and their restore was proven; the driver
				logged "Touch ID: restored N".
		empty		The enclave lists no enrolled fingerprint.
				Touch ID can enrol, and has nothing to match.
		unproven	The enclave lists fingerprints, but their
				restore could not be proven. Matching is
				refused this boot.
		========	============================================

		A tool deciding whether Touch ID is ready to match at boot
		reads touchid "ready", sensor_attach "attached", restore
		"restored" (or "empty", with nothing to match) and matching
		(not "blocked").

What:		/sys/bus/platform/devices/<unit>.sep/diag/xart_guard
Date:		October 2026
KernelVersion:	7.2
Contact:	Ryan Murray <ryan@aurorasilicon.org>
Description:
		(RO) The level of the xART guard, which keeps the enclave's
		writes to the shared gigalocker away from session records
		this Linux install did not create, as an unsigned decimal.
		It is the effective level, after the xart_guard parameter's
		default (-1) has taken the platform's. Fixed at probe.

		==	================================================
		0	No guard runs: xart_guard=0, the platform's
			default is 0, or xART writes are disabled (see
			xart).
		1	Writes and deletes of session records that
			existed at probe and are not on Linux's list of
			its own records are refused.
		2	As 1, and writes to the shared root records are
			refused too.
		==	================================================

What:		/sys/bus/platform/devices/<unit>.sep/diag/xart_guard_list
Date:		October 2026
KernelVersion:	7.2
Contact:	Ryan Murray <ryan@aurorasilicon.org>
Description:
		(RO) Whether the guard loaded Linux's list of the session
		records it created (/var/lib/aurora-sep-xart-owned.bin).
		Fixed at probe.

		===========	=========================================
		none		No guard runs (xart_guard reads "0").
		loaded		The list was read. An empty list is
				"loaded" too, and does not show that Touch
				ID was set up without the guard: a keybag
				created under it may write no record, and a
				rollback of the root file system can empty
				the list.
		unavailable	The list could not be opened or read. Every
				session record that existed at probe is
				protected and no new one can be adopted, so
				every session write is refused.
		===========	=========================================

What:		/sys/bus/platform/devices/<unit>.sep/diag/xart_guard_refusals
Date:		October 2026
KernelVersion:	7.2
Contact:	Ryan Murray <ryan@aurorasilicon.org>
Description:
		(RO) How many of the enclave's xART writes and deletes the
		guard has refused since probe, as an unsigned decimal. A
		new record that could not be added to Linux's list counts
		too, since its write is refused. Only the first few
		refusals are logged; this counts them all.

What:		/sys/bus/platform/devices/<unit>.sep/diag/matching
Date:		October 2026
KernelVersion:	7.2
Contact:	Ryan Murray <ryan@aurorasilicon.org>
Description:
		(RO) Whether a fingerprint match can save the state it
		writes to xART, as far as the driver can tell. A match
		rewrites session records that existed before it, so the xART
		guard can refuse it, and the verify then fails although
		the fingerprints were restored.

		==========	==========================================
		usable		xART writes are enabled and no guard runs.
		unverified	A guard runs with its list loaded and has
				refused nothing yet. Whether every record a
				match rewrites is on the list is not known
				until a match is tried.
		blocked		A match's write is refused or known to be:
				xART writes are disabled (xart "disabled"),
				the guard has refused a write
				(xart_guard_refusals above 0), the guard
				has no list (xart_guard_list
				"unavailable").
		==========	==========================================

		For the guard cases the documented opt-out is xart_guard=0,
		which lets the enclave rewrite session records that another
		OS on the Mac may have created; see
		tools/aurora-sep/XART-SAFETY.md. During a boot the value
		can only move from "unverified" to "blocked", once the guard
		refuses a write.

What:           /sys/bus/platform/devices/.../complete_attach
Date:           November 2026
Contact:        Aurora Silicon maintainers
Description:
                Write-only, root-only (0200). Writing 1 completes an attach
                started with apple_sep.keystore_only=1: opens host state and
                the identity index on the real root, reopens xART writable
                with the guard policy, and publishes the biometric
                devices. Sensor activation still occurs on first device open,
                just as with a normal attach. No SEP registration is repeated.
                Successful completion is idempotent. Failed store opens may
                be retried. Returns EINVAL for other values, or if the driver
                was loaded without keystore_only. The read-only module
                parameter retains its load-time value after completion.

                With keystore_only=1, xART is read-only until
                complete_attach (keystore_only=2 below), provisioning waits
                for it, and no host-state or guard files are opened for
                writing. The initrd must carry the
                existing identity keybag and machine ref-key at their usual
                /var/lib paths; both are cached on first trusted-key use.
                An unseal needing an xART write fails with EBADMSG so userspace
                can use its passphrase fallback.

                With apple_sep.keystore_only=2 the initrd phase also serves a
                password unlock of a per-user key bag on /dev/sep-bio-rearm:
                only the state, import, unlock, lock and FDE unseal requests;
                every other request returns EAGAIN until complete_attach.
                Loading and unlocking a bag needs enclave writes to xART, so
                in this mode xART is writable, but only when the xART
                guard is off (its record list lives in host files on the real
                root). With a guard, xART stays read-only, the bag requests
                fail with EROFS and the volume falls back to its passphrase.

What:		/sys/bus/platform/devices/<unit>.sep/diag/policy
Date:		November 2026
KernelVersion:	7.2
Contact:	Satya Benson <satya@benson.earth>
Description:
		(RO, ABI 3) A space-separated key=value snapshot of the
		driver policy selected at probe: xart_writes (effective store
		writability), provision_keybag (always 1: the parameter was
		removed and the driver always provisions a missing keybag),
		xart_guard (effective level),
		trusted_keys, filevault_api, xarm_trace, refkey_v2 and
		identity_override (0 or 1). API flags describe the selected
		policy, not successful registration if that API later fails.

		identity_source is pending before the driver selects it, dt
		for /chosen/apfs-preboot-uuid, override for explicit parameters,
		or missing. It is updated at the actual identity decision.
		No UUID value, state, identity or key bytes are exposed.
		Reads only use cached facts and never contact the enclave.
		Module-parameter sysfs files need not exist; their absence
		is not evidence of a mismatched policy. Older diagnostic
		ABIs retain xart/xart_guard and kernel decision messages.

What:		/sys/bus/platform/devices/<unit>.sep/diag/attach_ms
What:		/sys/bus/platform/devices/<unit>.sep/diag/ready_ms
Date:		November 2026
KernelVersion:	7.3
Contact:	Ryan Murray <ryan@aurorasilicon.org>
Description:
		(RO) How long the bring-up took, as unsigned decimal
		milliseconds counted from probe on the boot-time clock.
		attach_ms is the time to the attach decision (see attach);
		ready_ms is the time to the end of the boot-time bring-up,
		when keystore is decided. Each reads "0" until its decision
		is made. A large ready_ms with keystore "open" is an enclave
		that was slow to advertise the key store; the driver waits
		up to 90 s for it.

What:		/sys/bus/platform/devices/<unit>.sep/diag/endpoint_names
Date:		November 2026
KernelVersion:	7.3
Contact:	Ryan Murray <ryan@aurorasilicon.org>
Description:
		(RO) The endpoints the SEP has advertised, as the
		four-character names their descriptors carry with trailing
		spaces dropped, separated by single spaces, in endpoint
		number order, for example "cntl sbio scrd sse hdcp xars sks
		xarm hibe pnon skdl stac". A name that is not lower-case
		letters and digits is shown as the endpoint number in two
		hex digits. Empty until the first endpoint is advertised;
		discovery goes on after the attach, so the list can still
		grow, like endpoints.

What:		/sys/bus/platform/devices/<unit>.sep/diag/os_identity
Date:		November 2026
KernelVersion:	7.3
Contact:	Ryan Murray <ryan@aurorasilicon.org>
Description:
		(RO) Where the OS identity that the driver gives the
		enclave's xART exchange came from. Decided at the attach.

		===========	=========================================
		unknown		Not decided yet.
		explicit	The os_uuid_hi and os_uuid_lo parameters.
		chosen		/chosen/apfs-preboot-uuid, as the boot
				loader passed it.
		unavailable	Neither; the enclave is told the identity
				is unavailable and its xART records for
				this OS cannot be found.
		===========	=========================================

What:		/sys/bus/platform/devices/<unit>.sep/diag/hwrng
Date:		November 2026
KernelVersion:	7.3
Contact:	Ryan Murray <ryan@aurorasilicon.org>
Description:
		(RO) The enclave's random number source. "pending" until
		the survey after the bring-up decides, then "registered"
		(it is an hwrng, named apple-sep) or "failed" (the survey
		gave up; the kernel log says why).

What:		/sys/bus/platform/devices/<unit>.sep/diag/stac
What:		/sys/bus/platform/devices/<unit>.sep/diag/stac_onlines
Date:		November 2026
KernelVersion:	7.3
Contact:	Ryan Murray <ryan@aurorasilicon.org>
Description:
		(RO) The enclave's trusted-accessory service (endpoint
		stac). The driver tells it the system is online once the
		bring-up ends and again after each wake of the enclave from
		a sleep it was told of, as macOS does; it sends the service
		nothing else. stac reads "pending" until the first report,
		then "online" (the service took it), "absent" (the enclave
		does not offer the endpoint with the buffer layout the
		driver knows, so it is left alone) or "failed" (the last
		report was refused, malformed, unanswered or not sent; the
		kernel log says which). stac_onlines counts the accepted
		reports since probe, as an unsigned decimal.

What:		/sys/bus/platform/devices/<unit>.sep/diag/pnon
What:		/sys/bus/platform/devices/<unit>.sep/diag/local_policy_digest
Date:		November 2026
KernelVersion:	7.3
Contact:	Ryan Murray <ryan@aurorasilicon.org>
Description:
		The enclave's boot-policy service (endpoint pnon). Once the
		bring-up ends, the driver asks it once for the SHA-384
		digest of the nonce the blessed LocalPolicy of this boot is
		bound to, and sends it nothing else. The nonce stays in the
		enclave and changes only when the policy is replaced, so the
		digest is the enclave's account of which LocalPolicy admitted
		this boot.
		(RO) pnon reads "pending" until the read ends, then "read",
		"absent" (the enclave does not offer the endpoint with a
		buffer layout the driver knows, so it is left alone) or
		"failed" (refused, malformed, unanswered or not sent; the
		kernel log says which).
		(RO, root only) local_policy_digest is the digest as 96
		lowercase hex digits once pnon reads "read", and
		"unavailable" before then.

What:		/sys/bus/platform/devices/<unit>.sep/diag/sse
What:		/sys/bus/platform/devices/<unit>.sep/diag/hibe
What:		/sys/bus/platform/devices/<unit>.sep/diag/skdl
What:		/sys/bus/platform/devices/<unit>.sep/diag/sse_events
Date:		November 2026
KernelVersion:	7.3
Contact:	Ryan Murray <ryan@aurorasilicon.org>
Description:
		Three enclave services Linux has no consumer for: Secure
		Enclave to Secure Element sharing (endpoint sse), secure
		hibernation (hibe) and the CoreKDL kernel deny-list (skdl).
		As macOS at boot, the driver checks that each is offered and
		sends it nothing.
		(RO) sse, hibe and skdl read "pending" until the bring-up
		ends, then "idle" (offered with its name and the buffer layout
		the driver knows) or "absent" (not offered, or with another
		name or layout).
		(RO) sse_events counts the events the secure-element sharing
		service announced on its own since probe, as an unsigned
		decimal. They are not answered.

What:		/sys/bus/platform/devices/<unit>.sep/diag/xarm_failures
Date:		November 2026
KernelVersion:	7.3
Contact:	Ryan Murray <ryan@aurorasilicon.org>
Description:
		(RO) How many of the enclave's xART requests the driver has
		answered with the failure status since probe, as an unsigned
		decimal: a store read or write that failed, a bad checksum,
		an unknown request, a buffer that was not ready, and the
		writes the xART guard refused (also counted in
		xart_guard_refusals). The first is logged; this counts them
		all. Anything above xart_guard_refusals is a failure that
		was not the guard's choice.

What:		/sys/bus/platform/devices/<unit>.sep/diag/unhandled_messages
Date:		November 2026
KernelVersion:	7.3
Contact:	Ryan Murray <ryan@aurorasilicon.org>
Description:
		(RO) How many messages arrived on an endpoint the driver has
		no handler for, as an unsigned decimal. The first few are
		logged. Normally 0.

What:		/sys/bus/platform/devices/<unit>.sep/diag/sleep
What:		/sys/bus/platform/devices/<unit>.sep/diag/sleeps
Date:		November 2026
KernelVersion:	7.3
Contact:	Ryan Murray <ryan@aurorasilicon.org>
Description:
		(RO) The driver's view of system sleep. sleep reads "held"
		from the PM notifier's prepare event to its post event,
		while Touch ID captures and entropy requests are held, and
		"awake" otherwise. sleeps counts completed prepare/post
		pairs since probe, aborted suspends included. The enclave's
		power domains stay on across sleep, so every other attribute
		keeps its value over a suspend; a reader checking that the
		enclave still answers after a resume reads the hwrng.
